// P2 (plan 22) — DOES A REQUEST/RESPONSE ROUND-TRIP TO JS WORK IN A `--html` PAGE?
//
// The page's own comment describes the pattern as first-class:
//
//   "loft -> JS structured messages (host_output()): the page handles them in
//    globalThis.loftOutput(msg) — the request/response pattern is host_output a
//    request, act on it in JS, loftPush the completion."
//
// Everything plan 22's `W5` rests on is that paragraph being true in a page we
// can actually build, with JS WE appended. If it is false, localStorage needs
// loft#851 and the interim shim is impossible.
//
// ⚠ THE CONTROL IS IN THE SAME RUN. `B` asks for a key JS does not answer, so a
// harness that echoed everything — or one where `host_input` simply returned the
// last thing written — would pass `A` and fail `B`. One exchange proves a wire;
// two prove it carries what was asked for.
fn ask(req: text) -> text {
  host_output(req);
  host_input()
}

fn main() {
  // A — the round trip. JS answers "ANS:" + the tail.
  a = ask("REQ:hello");
  println("P2 A got [{a}]");

  // B — the control: a request JS is written to IGNORE. The answer must be
  // EMPTY, not the previous answer and not an echo.
  b = ask("SKIP:hello");
  println("P2 B got [{b}]");

  // C — a second real exchange, to show the channel is not single-shot and that
  // `B` did not wedge it.
  c = ask("REQ:world");
  println("P2 C got [{c}]");

  ok_a = a == "ANS:hello";
  ok_b = b == "";
  ok_c = c == "ANS:world";
  if ok_a && ok_b && ok_c {
    println("P2 RESULT ok");
  } else {
    println("P2 RESULT bad a={ok_a} b={ok_b} c={ok_c}");
  }
}
